The copy assignment operator, which the copy constructor also calls, never copies `second`:

```cpp
gsAABB<d,Z>& operator= ( const gsAABB<d,Z>& other )
{
    if (this!=&other)
    {
        first  = other.first;
        level  = other.level;
    }
    return *this;
}
```

- **Copy constructor:** `gsAABB(const gsAABB&)` delegates to this operator, so a copy-constructed box has `second` default-constructed. For a fixed-size Eigen vector, that means uninitialized garbage.
- **Copy assignment:** assigning one box to another keeps the target's stale upper corner.

Any code that copies an AABB, such as pushing it into a `std::vector` or passing it by value, gets a box with the right lower corner and level but a wrong upper corner. That corrupts every bounding-box test built on it.

The move assignment operator does copy all three members (`first`, `second`, `level`), which shows `second` was meant to be included. This is a functional bug in the newly added code.

